;; talk/operator--canon.scm — objections to surfaces/operator.scm from the lens of canon.

(objection 'o-operator-canon-1 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'operator-default-auto-credit :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'credit-is-chosen "Credit is chosen by the parties, manually or through a policy they set. This default is the opposite: every customer who asks gets a line up to 1,000 dollars' worth per token from a cap the operator never chose, and d-operator-defaults leans to keeping it and only showing it. The two leanings disagree: d-operator-auto-credit leans to an operator-set cap, yet the surface installs the code cap first and for as long as the operator does not act. Showing a default is not the operator choosing it. The decision's own changes-if says the cap may be a testnet onboarding device, and testnet-is-not-real-funds says test results give no right to real money, so the surface assumes the cap goes away before real money without saying so. The cost is a hub whose unsecured exposure grows with every new account on a limit its operator did not set. Both decisions are the maintainer's; no decision in the file says the cap is waived on real money."))

(objection 'o-operator-canon-2 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'operator-owed-to-me :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'lending-reopened "Lending must appear with exact amounts, who holds authority, deadlines and recovery. This screen shows loan principal and interest so far only. The loan term and the late window are figures that exist in the ground and are not shown, and nothing says who can end a loan or what the operator does when a borrower does not repay on time. The same file is also split on authority: d-operator-launch-flows leans to terms-here, where approvals come from a policy or service, yet operator-credit-flow signs lend-approve, which is the approve-here option. The operator is told what is owed and not by when, nor who decides. The cost is a lender with a screen of balances and no deadline or recovery path for the money it has lent. Which of the two options carries authority needs a maintainer decision; d-operator-launch-flows is that decision and the flow does not follow its leaning."))

(objection 'o-operator-canon-3 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'operator-exits :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'recovery-times-shown-apart "A dispute view shows the response window, finalization eligibility and completed recovery as three separate things. This screen shows both response windows and the end time, but not exit-ready-at, the moment the case can first go to the chain, and it shows no completed-recovery outcome beyond what the answers line says the flow ends with. The first two blur into one end time, and the third is not on the screen. The operator's own signed time is one hour against a window of about twenty-five, so an operator who reads one end time reads the wrong deadline for their own answer. The cost is a missed answer read as a late one. The queue item is pending work, so the maintainer may still change it; nothing in the file says it has."))

(objection 'o-operator-canon-4 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'operator-needs-cover :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'recovery-coverage-visible "The release queue lists, as an item gating public launch, an account-level recovery coverage view with receipt and failure visibility for the watching service. The need asks the base for it, with a because of d-operator-disputes only, but no screen shows it: operator-needs-me, operator-customer and operator-exits do not list it, and no flow ends by telling the operator. Under errors-are-shown a failed job the screen never carries is a swallowed failure. The operator is the one person with thousands of accounts to cover, so the gap is largest here. The cost is an operator who believes a leaving customer will be answered because the need was written down, when the last job failed and nothing told them."))

(objection 'o-operator-canon-5 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'd-operator-launch-flows :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'launch-flows "A spec with several surfaces must say which surface carries each flow on each chain. This decision names swap, cross-chain swap and lending as carried on the operator's side, but the file names TRON only inside one :because of d-operator-scope, never in a screen, flow or default. It does not say on which chain the operator's lending terms or market opening apply, and a hub's terms can differ per chain. launch-flows-need-owning-tests also needs a browserless scenario and a browser test for every flow a surface offers, and none is named for operator-prices-flow, which signs three kinds of transaction at once. The cost is a launch flow left out of one chain without anyone having said so. The decision is the maintainer's, and nothing in the file shows the chain split being decided."))

(objection 'o-operator-canon-6 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'd-operator-reserve-short :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'no-stubs-in-flows "A flow either runs on the real runtime or is not offered; no stub, fake or hidden compromise stands in for it. The leaning is refund-at-timeout, and the decision's own text says no code refunds on the declared ten-minute timeout. operator-default-refund then lists a timeout as a default that does nothing, and operator-refill-flow ends with every paid request filled, waiting or refunded, which holds only if the runtime changes. A declared timeout that nothing enforces is a hidden compromise, and the paid fee stays with the hub meanwhile. The decider is already the maintainer and no answer exists, so the surface depends on a runtime change it does not name as a need. The cost is a customer who paid for locking and is told of a refund time that never comes."))
